ponds to the edge $r_i c_j$ of the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$ with bipartition $(R,C)$.  \\n\\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a *perfect matching*\\n\\\\[\\nM=\\\\{\\\\,r_i c_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\,\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\nwhere $\\\\pi$ is a permutation of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$.  \\n\\nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines and which does not contain an uncovered square corresponds precisely to a complete bipartite subgraph $K_{A,B}$ with $A\\\\subseteq R$, $B\\\\subseteq C$ and\\n\\\\[\\nA\\\\cap\\\\pi^{-1}(B)=\\\\varnothing .\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, condition (1) guarantees that no edge of $M$ belongs to $K_{A,B}$, i.e. the tile avoids every uncovered square.  The collection of tiles required in the problem is therefore a *partition* of the edge set $E(K_{n,n})\\\\setminus M$ into subgraphs of the form $K_{A,B}$ satisfying (1).\\n\\nConsequently the problem is to determine the smallest integer $k$ for which there exist subsets\\n\\\\[\\n(A_1,B_1),\\\\dots ,(A_k,B_k)\\\\qquad (A_t\\\\subseteq R,\\\\;B_t\\\\subseteq C)\\n\\\\]\\nwith (1) and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigcup_{t=1}^{k} A_t\\\\times B_t = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j\\\\neq\\\\pi(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nDenote this minimum by $f(n)$.  We shall prove $f(n)=2n-2$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**2.   A lower bound of $2n-2$.**  \\n\\nSplit the complement of the matching into two parts:\\n\\\\[\\nR=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad \\nL=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nClearly $R\\\\cup L$ is exactly the set on the right‑hand side of (2) and $R\\\\cap L=\\\\varnothing$.\\n\\n*Right part.*  \\nFor each column $j\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$ define\\n\\\\[\\nr(j)=\\\\min\\\\{\\\\,i\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<j\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $\\\\pi$ is a permutation, the set $\\\\{i\\\\mid\\\\pi(i)<j\\\\}$ is non‑empty, and $r(j)$ is well defined.  The cell $(r(j),j)$ belongs to $R$ and is the **topmost** cell of column $j$ inside $R$.\\n\\n\\\\textbf{Claim.}\\\\quad\\\\text{No rectangle $A_t\\\\times B_t$ can contain two distinct cells }$(r(j),j)$\\\\text{ and }$(r(k),k)$\\\\text{ with }$j<k$.\\n\\n*Proof of the claim.*\\nAssume a rectangle $A\\\\times B$ contains $(r(j),j)$ and $(r(k),k)$ with $j<k$.  Then $j,k\\\\in B$ and $r(j),r(k)\\\\in A$, so $A$ contains the whole interval $[r(j),r(k)]$ of rows and $B$ contains the whole interval $[j,k]$ of columns.  Consequently the rectangle also contains the cell $(r(j),k)$.  By definition of $r(j)$ we have $\\\\pi(r(j))\\\\ge k$, for otherwise $r(j)$ would not be the minimal index with $\\\\pi(i)<j$.  Hence $(r(j),k)$ lies **left** of the hole in row $r(j)$, i.e. $(r(j),k)\\\\notin R$.  This contradicts the assumption that the rectangle is contained in $R$.  ∎\\n\\nBecause there are $n-1$ columns $j\\\\ge2$, the claim shows that at least $n-1$ distinct rectangles are needed to cover $R$.\\n\\n*Left part.*  The argument for $L$ is symmetric.  For each column $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$ define\\n\\\\[\\nb(j)=\\\\max\\\\{\\\\,i\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)>j\\\\,\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\nso that $(b(j),j)$ is the **bottommost** cell of column $j$ inside $L$.  An identical “bottom‑cell” claim shows that at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover $L$.\\n\\nSince a rectangle cannot intersect both $R$ and $L$ (otherwise it would contain a hole), the total number of rectangles needed for the whole board is at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**3.   An explicit tiling with $2n-2$ rectangles.**  \\n\\nChoose any permutation $\\\\pi$; for concreteness we take the cyclic shift\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\pi(i)=\\\\begin{cases}\\ni+1 & (1\\\\le i<n),\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\n1   & (i=n).\\n\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n*Right‑hand rectangles.*  For every row $i$ with $\\\\pi(i)<n$ (i.e. for all rows except the one whose uncovered square lies in the last column) place the rectangle\\n\\\\[\\nR_i=\\\\{\\\\,i\\\\,\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n$R_i$ has height $1$ and width $n-\\\\pi(i)$; it consists of all cells to the right of the uncovered cell in row $i$.\\n\\n*Below‑hand rectangles.*  For every column $j$ with $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)<n$ (i.e. for all columns except the one whose uncovered square lies in the first row) place the rectangle\\n\\\\[\\nC_j=\\\\{\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\,j\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n$C_j$ has width $1$ and height $n-\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$; it consists of all cells below the uncovered cell in column $j$.\\n\\nThere are exactly $n-1$ rectangles of type (5) and $n-1$ rectangles of type (6), so the total number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\n*Verification that the tiles are disjoint and cover everything.*  \\nLet $(i,j)$ be a cell that is not a hole, i.e. $j\\\\neq\\\\pi(i)$.  Exactly one of the two strict inequalities holds:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{either }j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, if both $j<\\\\pi(i)$ and $i<\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$ held, applying $\\\\pi$ to the second inequality would give $\\\\pi(i)<j$, contradicting $j<\\\\pi(i)$.  Hence (7) is true.\\n\\n- If $j>\\\\pi(i)$, then by definition $j\\\\in\\\\{\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$, so $(i,j)\\\\in R_i$.\\n- If $i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$, then $i\\\\in\\\\{\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$, so $(i,j)\\\\in C_j$.\\nThus every admissible cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles (5)–(6).  No rectangle contains a hole, because each $R_i$ uses only columns larger than $\\\\pi(i)$ and each $C_j$ uses only rows larger than $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$.  Consequently the family of $2n-2$ rectangles is a valid tiling.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**4.   Conclusion.**  \\nThe lower bound (3) shows that any tiling must use at least $2n-2$ tiles.  The construction of Section 3 exhibits a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\nf(n)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\nf(2025)=2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with $4048$ tiles, and it is impossible to do so with fewer.\\n\\n---\\n**Answer:** $\\\\boxed{4048}$\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
