{
    "solution": "Let $n=2025$.  We have an $n\\times n$ board of unit squares.  Matilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  Hence the set $U$ of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there is a bijection $\\pi:\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ such that\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots,n\\}.\n\\]\nThe remaining $n^2-n$ squares have to be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles, each tile being a set of whole unit squares and no two tiles overlapping.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of tiles\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor a row $i$ denote by $c_i=\\pi(i)$ the column of the uncovered square in that row.  The uncovered square splits row $i$ into a left part $L_i$ consisting of the squares $(i,1),\\dots,(i,c_i-1)$ and a right part $R_i$ consisting of the squares $(i,c_i+1),\\dots,(i,n)$.  Any tile that contains a square of row $i$ must be completely contained either in $L_i$ or in $R_i$, because a tile cannot contain the uncovered square.\n\nDefine a *left\u2011segment* to be a maximal set of consecutive rows $i,i+1,\\dots,j$ for which the intervals $[1,c_k-1]$ (the columns belonging to $L_k$) have a non\u2011empty common intersection.  In other words, there is a column $x$ such that $x<c_k$ for every $k\\in\\{i,\\dots,j\\}$.  The squares of all rows $i,\\dots,j$ that lie in column $x$ form a vertical line of unit squares that is completely free of uncovered squares; therefore the whole rectangle consisting of rows $i\\dots j$ and columns $1\\dots x$ can be covered by a single tile.  The same definition works for *right\u2011segments* (using the intervals $[c_k+1,n]$).\n\nObserve that the left\u2011segments partition the set of rows into at most $n-1$ pieces: whenever we move from row $i$ to row $i+1$, either $c_{i+1}>c_i$ (the uncovered column moves to the right) or $c_{i+1}<c_i$ (it moves to the left).  Each time the direction changes we start a new left\u2011segment.  Since the sequence $c_1,c_2,\\dots,c_n$ can change direction at most $n-2$ times, there are at most $n-1$ left\u2011segments.  An analogous argument shows that there are at most $n-1$ right\u2011segments.\n\nEvery tile must lie completely inside a left\u2011segment or completely inside a right\u2011segment, because a tile that crossed from a left\u2011part of a row to a right\u2011part would have to contain the uncovered square of that row.  Consequently the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\n\\[\nT\\ge (\\text{number of left\u2011segments})+(\\text{number of right\u2011segments})\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus **any** admissible tiling uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nTake the *identity* permutation $\\pi(i)=i$; the uncovered squares are the main diagonal $(i,i)$.  The board is then split into two stair\u2011shaped regions:\n* the *lower* region $\\{(r,c)\\mid r>c\\}$ (strictly below the diagonal), and\n* the *upper* region $\\{(r,c)\\mid r<c\\}$ (strictly above the diagonal).\nBoth regions can be tiled by $n-1$ rectangles.\n\n*Lower region.*  For each column $c$ with $1\\le c\\le n-1$ take the rectangle consisting of all squares in column $c$ whose row index is larger than $c$, i.e. the set\n\\[\n\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots,n\\}.\n\\]\nThese $n-1$ rectangles are disjoint and together cover the whole lower region.\n\n*Upper region.*  Symmetrically, for each row $r$ with $1\\le r\\le n-1$ take the rectangle consisting of all squares in row $r$ whose column index is larger than $r$, i.e.\n\\[\n\\{(r,c)\\mid c=r+1,\\dots,n\\}.\n\\]\nAgain we obtain $n-1$ disjoint rectangles covering the whole upper region.\n\nThus the complement of the diagonal (the $n^2-n$ squares that must be covered) is partitioned into exactly\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\]\nrectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe lower bound proved in Section\u202f1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while the explicit construction of Section\u202f2 uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Hence the bound is tight and the construction is optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Answer for $n=2025$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPutting $n=2025$ we obtain the minimal possible number of tiles\n\\[\nT_{\\min}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  The minimum number of rectangular tiles required is $\\boxed{4048}$.\n",
    "final_answer": "4048"
}
