riors disjoint) needs at least \\(k\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  Order the distinct positive lengths as\n\\[\\ell_1 > \\ell_2 > \\dots > \\ell_k .\\]\nConsider the column numbered \\(\\ell_j\\) (the \\(\\ell_j\\)-th column from the left).  The rows that contain a cell in this column are precisely those rows whose length is at least \\(\\ell_j\\).  Among them, the rows whose length equals \\(\\ell_j\\) have this column as their rightmost cell.\nAny rectangle that covers a cell in column \\(\\ell_j\\) cannot extend beyond column \\(\\ell_j\\); otherwise it would contain a cell that does not belong to the Ferrers board.  Consequently, a rectangle covering the cells of column \\(\\ell_j\\) can involve only rows whose length is **at least** \\(\\ell_j\\).  In particular, all rows of length exactly \\(\\ell_j\\) must be covered by rectangles whose right side is column \\(\\ell_j\\).\nIf two different lengths \\(\\ell_j\\) and \\(\\ell_{j'}\\) (with \\(j\\neq j'\\)) were covered by the same rectangle, that rectangle would have to end at the smaller of the two rightmost columns, contradicting the fact that it must also contain a cell in the larger column.  Hence the cells in column \\(\\ell_j\\) require a rectangle that is dedicated to that column.  Distinct \\(\\ell_j\\) therefore force distinct rectangles, giving at least \\(k\\) rectangles.  \u220e\n\n#### 4. Lemma 2 \u2013 Application to the complement of a permutation\n\n*Lemma 2.*  For any permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the complement of its permutation matrix needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  As observed in Section&nbsp;2, the left part \\(F_L\\) is a Ferrers board whose positive row lengths are the \\(n-1\\) distinct numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  By Lemma&nbsp;1, covering \\(F_L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\nThe right part \\(F_R\\) is the transpose Ferrers board; its positive column lengths are again \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  Applying Lemma&nbsp;1 to \\(F_R\\) (or equiv