"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have obtained a complete solution. The minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is\\n\\\\[\\\\boxed{4048}.\\\\]\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n1. *Reformulation.* The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection $p:\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\to\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.\\n2. *Left and right parts.* For each row $i$ let $L_i=p(i)-1$ (squares to the left of the uncovered one) and $R_i=n-p(i)$ (squares to the right). The set of left squares forms a Ferrers board $F_L$ whose row lengths are $\\{L_i\\\\}$; the set of right squares forms the transpose Ferrers board $F_R$ whose column lengths are $\\{R_i\\\\}$.\\n3. *Lemma\u202f1 (Ferrers lower bound).* If a Ferrers board has $k$ distinct positive row lengths, any tiling of it by axis\u2011parallel rectangles (with interiors disjoint) needs at least $k$ rectangles.\\n4. *Lemma\u202f2 (Application to the complement of a permutation).* Because $\\{L_i\\\\}=\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ and $\\{R_i\\\\}=\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$, each of $F_L$ and $F_R$ has $n-1$ distinct positive lengths. Hence at least $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ rectangles are required to cover the whole complement of the permutation matrix.\\n5. *Construction attaining the bound.* Choose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$.  Then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal.  Cover the squares strictly above the diagonal by the $n-1$ horizontal rectangles\\n   $$H_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j=i+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\qquad i=1,\\\\dots,n-1,$$ \\n   and the squares strictly below the diagonal by the $n-1$ vertical rectangles\\n   $$V_j=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i=j+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\qquad j=1,\\\\dots,n-1.$$\\n   These $2n-2$ rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every unit square except the $n$ diagonal squares.\\n6. *Conclusion.* The lower bound $2n-2$ is tight, so the minimum number of tiles is $2n-2$.  For $n=2025$ this equals $4048$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  We work in general for an $n\\\\times n$ board and specialise at the end.\\n\\n#### 1. Reformulation as a permutation matrix\\n\\nWe must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  Hence the set $U$ of uncovered squares is a set of $n$ positions $(i,p(i))$, where $p$ is a permutation of $\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  All other squares must be covered by pairwise disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) whose sides lie on grid lines.\\n\\n#### 2. Decomposition into left and right parts\\n\\nFor each row $i$ define\\n\\\\[L_i = p(i)-1,\\\\qquad R_i = n-p(i).\\\\]\\nThe $L_i$ squares to the left of $(i,p(i))$ constitute the *left part* of row $i$; the $R_i$ squares to its right constitute the *right part*.\\nCollecting the left parts of all rows yields a Ferrers board $F_L$: its $i$\u2011th row (counted from the top) has length $L_i$.  Because $p$ is a permutation, the multiset $\\{L_i\\\\mid i=1\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ equals $\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$.  Consequently the positive row lengths of $F_L$ are precisely the $n-1$ distinct numbers $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$.\\nSimilarly, the right parts form the transpose Ferrers board $F_R$: its $j$\u2011th column (counted from the left) has length $R_j$, and $\\{R_j\\\\}=\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$.  Thus $F_R$ also has $n-1$ distinct positive column lengths.\\nThe complement of the permutation matrix is exactly $F_L\\\\cup F_R$, a disjoint union of the two Ferrers shapes.\\n\\n#### 3. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Ferrers lower bound\\n\\n**Lemma\u202f1.** *Let $F$ be a Ferrers board whose rows have $k$ distinct positive lengths.  Any covering of $F$ by axis\u2011parallel rectangles with pairwise disjoint interiors requires at least $k$ rectangles.*\\n\\n*Proof.*  List the distinct positive row lengths in decreasing order:\\n\\\\[\\\\ell_1>\\\\ell_2>\\\\dots>\\\\ell_k.\\\\]\\nFor each $\\\\ell_j$ consider column $\\\\ell_j$ (the $\\\\ell_j$\u2011th column from the left).  Because $\\\\ell_j$ is a row length, there exists at least one row $i$ with $L_i=\\\\ell_j$, and the cell $(i,\\\\ell_j)$ is the rightmost cell of that row.  Any rectangle covering this cell cannot extend beyond column $\\\\ell_j$, for otherwise it would contain a cell not belonging to $F$.  Hence every rectangle that contains any cell of column $\\\\ell_j$ has its right side exactly at column $\\\\ell_j$.\\nIf $j\\\\neq j'$, then $\\\\ell_{j'}>\\\\ell_j$ implies column $\\\\ell_{j'}$ lies strictly to the right of column $\\\\ell_j$.  A rectangle whose right side is at column $\\\\ell_j$ cannot also cover a cell in column $\\\\ell_{j'}$.  Therefore the cells of column $\\\\ell_j$ must be covered by rectangles that are distinct from those covering the cells of column $\\\\ell_{j'}$.  Since there are $k$ such columns, at least $k$ rectangles are needed.  \u220e\\n\\n#### 4. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Application to the complement of a permutation\\n\\n**Lemma\u202f2.** *For any permutation $p$ of $\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$, covering the complement of its permutation matrix with disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles requires at least $2n-2$ rectangles.*\\n\\n*Proof.*  As observed, the left part $F_L$ is a Ferrers board whose positive row lengths are $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$, i.e. $n-1$ distinct values.  By Lemma\u202f1, $F_L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.\\nThe right part $F_R$ is not a Ferrers board in the row direction, but its column lengths are $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$.  Rotating $F_R$ by $90^{\\\\circ}$ turns it into a Ferrers board with $n-1$ distinct positive row lengths, so Lemma\u202f1 applied to the rotated shape shows that $F_R$ also needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.\\nSince $F_L$ and $F_R$ are disjoint, any tiling of their union needs at least $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ rectangles.  \u220e\\n\\n#### 5. Construction attaining the lower bound\\n\\nTake the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ for all $i$.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, $i=1,\\\\dots,n$.\\nDefine the following $2n-2$ rectangles:\\n\\n*For each $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$, let\\n\\\\[H_i = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j=i+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\\\]\\n$H_i$ is a $1\\\\times (n-i)$ horizontal strip covering all squares in row $i$ strictly to the right of the diagonal.\\n\\n*For each $j=1,\\\\dots,n-1$, let\\n\\\\[V_j = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i=j+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\\\]\\n$V_j$ is a $(n-j)\\\\times 1$ vertical strip covering all squares in column $j$ strictly below the diagonal.\\n\\nThe sets $H_i$ are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows; the sets $V_j$ are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct columns; and $H_i\\\\cap V_j=\\\\emptyset$ for all $i,j$, because $H_i$ consists of cells with column $>i$ while $V_j$ consists of cells with row $>j$, and the inequality $j>i$ (or $i>j$) never holds simultaneously for a cell to belong to both.  Consequently the $2n-2$ rectangles are disjoint.\\nEvery unit square not on the diagonal belongs either to some $H_i$ (if it lies above the diagonal) or to some $V_j$ (if it lies below the diagonal).  Hence the $2n-2$ rectangles cover all squares except the $n$ diagonal squares, exactly as required.\\nThus the lower bound $2n-2$ is achievable.\\n\\n#### 6. Conclusion for $n=2025$\\n\\nSubstituting $n=2025$ into the formula $2n-2$ yields\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\\\]\\nTherefore the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is **4048**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\\n\\nBefore finalizing, I reviewed the Summary and Detailed Solution to ensure:\\n1. All definitions (permutation matrix, left/right parts, Ferrers board) are explicit.\\n2. Lemma\u202f1 is proved rigorously with a clear argument about rightmost columns forcing distinct rectangles.\\n3. Lemma\u202f2 correctly applies Lemma\u202f1 to both the left and right parts (using transpose for the right part).\\n4. The construction for the identity permutation is described precisely, with verification that the rectangles are disjoint and cover exactly the required squares.\\n5. The final numerical answer is computed correctly as 4048.\\nAll steps are logically sound and no unjustified jumps remain.\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
