n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2=\\\\boxed{4048}\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 2.  Why no construction can use fewer tiles\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWe prove that any placement satisfying the condition must use at least \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles.\\n\\n*Step 1 – holes in the first and last columns.*\\nBecause each column contains exactly one uncovered square, there is precisely one hole in column 1 and one hole in column $n$.  Those two holes lie on the border of the board; consequently the squares in column 1 (except the hole) can be covered by a single vertical rectangle, and the same holds for column $n$.\\n\\n*Step 2 – interior holes force a left‑hand and a right‑hand piece.*\\nConsider a column \\\\(c\\\\) with \\\\(2\\\\le c\\\\le n-1\\\\).  Let the hole in this column be at row \\\\(r\\\\) (so the uncovered square is \\\\((r,c)\\\\)).  In row \\\\(r\\\\) the hole splits the row into a non‑empty left part (columns \\\\(1,\\\\dots ,c-1\\\\)) and a non‑empty right part (columns \\\\(c+1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\)).  No tile can contain both parts, because a rectangle that contains the left part would also contain the square \\\\((r,c)\\\\), which is forbidden.  Hence at least one tile is needed to cover the left part of row \\\\(r\\\\) and at least one (different) tile is needed to cover the right part of row \\\\(r\\\\).\\n\\nThus every interior column contributes **at least two** distinct tiles: one covering the squares to the left of its hole, and one covering the squares to the right of its hole.\\n\\n*Step 3 – counting the lower bound.*\\nThere are \\\\(n-2\\\\) interior columns.  From Step 2 we obtain at least \\\\(2(n-2)\\\\) tiles.  Adding the two tiles that cover the non‑interior columns (Step 1) gives a total of at least\\n\\\\[\\n2(n-2)+2 = 2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 3.  Conclusion\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe construction of Section 1 uses exactly \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles, and the argument of Section 2 shows that no arrangement can use fewer.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe solution relies on a combinatorial partition of the board into vertical and horizontal strips and a counting argument based on the forced left‑right split created by each interior uncovered square; it does not employ the diagonal‑permutation, Ferrers‑diagram, or greedy methods previously ruled out.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
