that the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $p(i)$.  We write the hole in row $i$ as $H_i=(i,p(i))$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Two families of special squares\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor every row $i$ define\n\\[\nL_i =\\begin{cases}(i,p(i)-1),&p(i)>1,\\\\[2pt]\\text{undefined},&p(i)=1,\\end{cases}\\qquad\nR_i =\\begin{cases}(i,p(i)+1),&p(i)<n,\\\\[2pt]\\text{undefined},&p(i)=n.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe squares $L_i$ are the rightmost squares **to the left** of the hole in row $i$; the squares $R_i$ are the leftmost squares **to the right** of the hole in row $i$.  Exactly one row has $p(i)=1$, so there are $n-1$ defined $L_i$; similarly there are $n-1$ defined $R_i$.  We call the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal S = \\{L_i : p(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i : p(i)<n\\}\n\\]\nthe set of *special squares*; $|\\mathcal S|=2n-2$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tile can contain at most one special square\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nA rectangular tile $T$ is determined by a non\u2011empty interval of rows $I=[a,b]$ and a non\u2011empty interval of columns $J=[c,d]$; it covers exactly the squares $(i,j)$ with $i\\in I$ and $j\\in J$.  By definition $T$ may not contain any hole $H_i$.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  $T$ cannot contain two distinct squares $L_i$ and $L_k$.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose $i<k$ and $T$ contains $L_i=(i,p(i)-1)$ and $L_k=(k,p(k)-1)$.  Then $c\\le p(i)-1$ and $c\\le p(k)-1$, and $d\\ge p(i)-1$ and $d\\ge p(k)-1$.  If $p(i)\\le p(k)$, then $d\\ge p(k)-1\\ge p(i)$, so column $p(i)$ belongs to $J$; because $i\\in I$, the hole $H_i=(i,p(i))$ would lie in $T$, a contradiction.  If $p(i)>p(k)$, then $d\\ge p(i)-1\\ge p(k)$, so column $p(k)$ belongs to $J$ and $k\\in I$, again forcing $H_k$ into $T$.  Hence no such pair can exist.  $\\square$\n\n*Lemma 2.*  $T$ cannot contain two distinct squares $R_i$ and $R_k$.\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to Lemma\u00a01, using the columns $p(i)+1$ and $p(k)+1$.  $\\square$\n\n*Lemma 3.*  $T$ cannot contain a square $L_i$ and a square $R_k$ (possibly with $i=k$).\n\n*Proof.*  Assume $T$ contains $L_i=(i,p(i)-1)$ and $R_k=(k,p(k)+1)$.  Then $c\\le p(i)-1$ and $d\\ge p(k)+1$.  If $p(i)\\le p(k)$, then $d\\ge p(k)+1>p(k)\\ge p(i)$, so column $p(i)$ lies in $J$ and $i\\in I$, implying $H_i\\in T$.  If $p(i)>p(k)$, then $c\\le p(i)-1\\ge p(k)$, so column $p(k)$ lies in $J$ and $k\\in I$, implying $H_k\\in T$.  In either case a hole would be covered, contradicting the rules.  $\\square$\n\nFrom Lemmas\u00a01\u20133 we conclude that **each tile can contain at most one element of $\\mathcal S$**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince $|\\mathcal S|=2n-2$, the previous paragraph forces any admissible placement to use at least $2n-2$ tiles:\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge |\\mathcal S| = 2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this gives the bound $\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 4048$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nDefine the permutation\n\\[\n p(i)=\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2pt]1,&i=n.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus the holes are\n\\[\nH_i=(i,i+1)\\quad (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\qquad H_n=(n,1).\n\\]\nWe place the following $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*\n\\begin{itemize}\n  \\item Column $1$: one tile covering rows $1,2,\\dots ,n-1$.\n  \\item For each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n-1$, a tile covering rows $j,j+1,\\dots ,n-1$ in column $j$.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese are $n-1$ tiles.  None of them contains a hole, because the hole in column $j$ lies at row $j-1$, which is strictly above the top row $j$ of the tile.\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*\n\\begin{