{
    "solution": "Let $n=2025$ and let $\\sigma$ be the permutation that gives the column of the uncovered square in each row; i.e. the uncovered squares are\n\\[(1,\\sigma(1)),(2,\\sigma(2)),\\dots,(n,\\sigma(n)).\\]\nAll other unit squares have to be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A useful description\nFor a set $R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ of rows denote\n\\[c(R)=\\{\\sigma(i):i\\in R\\}.\\]\nIf a tile uses exactly the rows $R$, then its column interval must be a set $C$ that does **not** meet $c(R)$; otherwise the tile would contain a forbidden square.  Consequently\n\\[|C|\\le n-|R|.\\]\nThus a tile covering $|R|$ rows can cover at most $|R|(n-|R|)$ unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound of $2n-2$\nConsider any tiling that satisfies the conditions.  Split the tiles into two families:\n* **left tiles** \u2013 those whose column interval lies completely to the left of the uncovered square in every row they occupy;\n* **right tiles** \u2013 those whose column interval lies completely to the right of the uncovered square in every row they occupy.\nA tile cannot be both left and right, because the uncovered squares lie in distinct columns.\n\nFor a left tile let $r$ be the number of rows it occupies.  As observed above, the tile can use at most $n-r$ columns, all of them situated left of the $r$ holes that belong to its rows.  Hence the tile leaves at least $r$ columns uncovered (the $r$ columns $\\sigma(i)$ of its rows).  The same statement holds for a right tile.\n\nNow count the uncovered columns in two different ways.\n* Every row contributes **exactly one** uncovered column, namely $\\sigma(i)$.  Hence the total number of uncovered columns is $n$.\n* On the other hand, each left (resp. right) tile that occupies $r$ rows leaves at least $r$ uncovered columns, and these columns are distinct for different tiles because the interiors of the tiles are disjoint.\n  Therefore the sum of the numbers of rows covered by all left tiles is at most $n$, and the same holds for the right tiles.\n\nIf the tiling uses $L$ left tiles and $R$ right tiles, then\n\\[\\sum_{\\text{left tiles}} r \\le n,\\qquad \\sum_{\\text{right tiles}} r \\le n.\n\\]\nSince each tile occupies at least one row, we obtain\n\\[L \\ge n-1,\\qquad R \\ge n-1.\\]\nConsequently the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\n\\[T = L+R \\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\]\nThus **any** admissible placement needs at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A construction with $2n-2$ tiles\nWe now show that $2n-2$ tiles are sufficient, proving the lower bound to be tight.\nArrange the rows in the natural order $1,2,\\dots,n$.\n\n*For the left side.*\nFor each $k=1,2,\\dots,n-1$ place a tile\n\\[T_k^{\\text{L}} = \\{k,k+1,\\dots,n\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots,\\sigma(k)-1\\}.\n\\]\nThe column interval $\\{1,\\dots,\\sigma(k)-1\\}$ lies strictly left of the uncovered square $(k,\\sigma(k))$, and because $\\sigma(k)\\le \\sigma(i)$ for every $i\\ge k$, the same interval is also left of the uncovered squares in all rows $i\\ge k$.  Hence $T_k^{\\text{L}}$ contains no forbidden square.\nThese $n-1$ tiles are pairwise disjoint because their row sets are nested and their column intervals are nested in the opposite direction.\n\n*For the right side.*\nFor each $k=2,3,\\dots,n$ place a tile\n\\[T_k^{\\text{R}} = \\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}\\times\\{\\sigma(k)+1,\\sigma(k)+2,\\dots,n\\}.\n\\]\nNow the column interval lies strictly right of the uncovered square $(k,\\sigma(k))$, and for every $i\\le k$ we have $\\sigma(i)\\le \\sigma(k)$, so the interval is also right of the uncovered squares in rows $1,\\dots,k$.  Again no forbidden square is covered, and the $n-1$ tiles are disjoint.\n\nIt is easy to verify that every unit square that is not one of the $n$ uncovered squares belongs to exactly one of the $2n-2$ tiles described above.  Hence a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion for $n=2025$\nPutting $n=2025$ into the formula $2n-2$ we obtain\n\\[2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is $\\boxed{4048}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** 4048",
